rom ep-exdos13.rom page 0 cpu z80
default observed

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The rest of the name-field copy that starts at $14A5 (C = the field length, HL = where it goes)

@14A7 NAMEF_FULL
  The field is full: C goes back to 1, so the characters that follow are read and dropped.
  .14A7 C = 1: the next stored character only counts down

@14A8 NAMEF_NEXT
  Takes the next character of the name text into the field at HL.
  .14A8 A = the next character (upper case); Z at the end
  .14AB End of the text? Pad the field
  .14AD A separator?
  .14B0 Yes: give it back and pad the field
  .14B2 "*"?
  .14B6 "?"?

@14BA NAMEF_WILD
  Marks the name as holding a wildcard (B bit 5).

@14BC NAMEF_STORE
  Stores the character, unless the field is full.
  .14BC B bit 0: the name has a character
  .14BE D = 1: this field has a character
  .14C0 Field full? Drop the character
  .14C3 Store it

@14C7 NAMEF_STAR
  "*": fills the rest of the field with "?" and marks a wildcard.

@14C8 NAMEF_STARFILL
  Writes "?" until one position is left in C's count.
  .14C9 Last position? Mark the wildcard and go on
  .14CC Fill with "?"

@14D1 NAMEF_UNREAD
  Gives the separator back to the text, then pads the field.
  .14D1 Step the text pointer back over it

@14D4 NAMEF_PAD
  Pads the rest of the field with spaces and returns Z.
  .14D5 Field done: return Z

@14E4 NOT_LETTER
  Not an upper-case letter: returns NZ and NC (A is not 0 here).

@14EC SEP_ALL19
  Searches all 19 separator characters at $1500 for A.
  .14EE The 19 characters
  .14F3 No caller: the same search for space and tab only
  .14F5 Space and tab, the table's first two

@14F8 SEP_SEARCH
  Searches the separator table at $1500 for A; Z when A is in it.
  .14F8 HL = the separator characters
  .14FB Z when found

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The character filter of the name reader at $1513 (it runs in the other register set)

@1527 NCH_FILTER
  The next character of the text: 0 ends it (Z); C bit 1 asks for it unchanged; otherwise lower
  case becomes upper case and "!" or "'" become "\".
  .1527 End of the text?
  .152A Step past the character
  .152B Raw characters wanted?
  .152F Below "a"?
  .1533 Above "z"?
  .1537 Upper case

@1539 NCH_SEPCHK
  "!" and "'" stand for the "\" separator.
  .1539 "!"?
  .153D "'"?

@1541 NCH_BACKSLASH
  Replaces the character with "\".

@1543 NCH_GOT
  Returns the character with NZ and NC.

@1544 NCH_EXIT
  Back to the caller's register set.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Walking a drive's stored current directory again (from $154A)

@156C PATH_REWALK
  Starts at the root and walks the drive's stored path text component by component, with the
  disk searched when no cluster was stored.
  .156C Save the caller's BC and DE
  .156F Begin at the root, the path text empty

@1573 PATH_REWALK_LP
  One component: parse it, find it, and go on while the text has more.
  .1573 Parse the next component into the 11-byte name
  .1576 Append it to the path text and find it
  .1579 Failed?
  .157C Text left?
  .157F Yes: the next component
  .1581 Never jumps (Z here): hides LD D,$FF at $1582, reached from $157A
  .1584 Restore the caller's BC
  .1586 Restore the other set's DE and BC
  .1589 HL = the drive block's directory word + 1
  .158A Store the directory's cluster in the drive block
  .158D Walked it all? Make it the current directory
  .158F Failed: empty the stored path text
  .1593 Then start again at the root

@1594 DIR_SET_ROOT
  Makes the root the current directory and empties the path text being built.
  1. Puts ":" after the 64-byte path text at IY+$06DE (the end mark) and points HL' one byte
     before the text.
  2. Sets the current directory word (IY+$30) to $FF00 + E: the root.
  3. When C' bit 2 is set and that word equals IY+$5E's, sets IY+$5D bit 6.
  Entry: E = the low byte for the directory word; C' bit 2 = compare with IY+$5E.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; HL' = IY+$06DD; IY+$30-$31 = $FF00 + E.
  Error: none (every path returns A = 0).
  Changes: AF, D, HL'.
  Keeps: BC, E, HL, IX, IY.

@1595 DIR_ROOT_BODY
  The body of DIR_SET_ROOT; $154D and $1558 also enter here, with HL pushed.
  .1597 HL' = IY + $071E: the byte after the path text
  .159E ":" marks the end of the path text
  .15A0 HL' = IY + $06DD: the text's start - 1

@15A6 DIR_ROOT_WORD
  D = $FF: the directory word names the root.

@15A8 DIR_SET_CUR
  Stores DE as the current directory (IY+$30) and returns A = 0.
  .15A8 The current directory's cluster
  .15AF Compare it with IY+$5E?
  .15B4 HL = the word at IY+$5E
  .15BB The same directory?
  .15BF Yes: IY+$5D bit 6

@15C3 DIR_SET_DONE
  Returns A = 0 and Z.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One path component

@15D4 PATH_STEP
  Adds one parsed path component to the path text and, when asked, finds it on the disk.
  1. "." keeps the directory, ".." cuts the text back to the separator before the last name,
     any other name is appended as "\" + name, then "." + extension when it has one.
  2. A name with a wildcard, or without B bit 3, is refused.
  3. When C' bit 7 is set, the current directory is searched for a directory of that name,
     which becomes the current directory (IY+$30).
  Entry: B = the component's flags from the parse (bit 3 a name, bit 4 an extension, bit 5 a
  wildcard, bit 6 "." or "..", bit 7 ".."); the name at IY+$06D1 (11 bytes); HL' = the end of
  the path text; C' bit 7 = search the disk; HL = the drive parameter block.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; HL' = the new end of the path text; when searched, DE = the found entry.
  Error: A = $A8 ("Invalid pathname string": the text is full, or the name is not allowed)
  or $AA ("Directory not found"), NZ.
  Changes: AF, DE, all alternates; the scan state IY+$30-$3B.
  Keeps: BC, HL, IX, IY.
  .15D5 HL = the 11-byte name
  .15D9 Keep the caller's DE, restore HL
  .15DA Skips PUSH DE at $15DB, an entry from $3C65
  .15DC A = the component's flags
  .15DE Save the text pointers
  .15E0 DE' = the end of the path text
  .15E1 C' = the flags
  .15E2 "." or ".."?
  .15E6 ".."?

@15EA PATH_UP_SCAN
  "..": steps back through the path text to the separator before the last name.
  .15ED A = the separator character
  .15F2 Reached it?
  .15F5 The start of the text?

@15F8 PATH_TOO_LONG
  The path text is full: error $A8.
  .15F9 Restore the text pointers

@15FB PATH_BAD_NAME
  Returns error $A8 ("Invalid pathname string").
  .15FE Restore the caller's DE

@1600 PATH_APPEND
  Appends "\" + the name (spaces dropped), then "." + the extension when there is one.
  .1603 HL = the 11-byte name
  .1604 The separator and up to 8 name characters
  .1606 A = the separator character
  .160C The text full?
  .160E An extension?
  .1610 "." and up to 3 extension characters
  .1617 The text full?

@161A PATH_STEP_CHK
  Checks the component's flags and, when asked, searches the disk for it.
  .161A HL' = the end of the path text
  .161B A = the component's flags
  .161C Restore DE' and C' (bit 7: search the disk)
  .161E A name (bit 3) without a wildcard (bit 5)?
  .1624 Search the disk?
  .1628 No: done, A = 0
  .1629 Start the scan of the current directory

@162C PATH_FIND_LP
  Compares each entry with the name until a directory of that name is found.
  .162C The end of the directory?
  .1630 HL = the 11-byte name
  .1633 Does the entry match?
  .163A A directory (attribute bit 4)?

@163E PATH_FIND_NEXT
  Steps to the next entry; C at the end of the directory.

@1643 PATH_NOT_FOUND
  Returns error $AA ("Directory not found").

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copying a name into the path text

@1656 COPY_NAME_LP
  Copies the name's next character, skipping spaces.
  .1658 Spaces are dropped

@165C COPY_NAME_PUT
  Stores A and copies up to B - 1 more characters from the name at HL into the path text at DE,
  skipping spaces; stops when the text is full (the ":" end mark is next).
  Entry: A = the first character to store; B = 1 + the characters to copy; HL = the name;
  DE = where the text goes.
  Exit: NZ, NC; DE = past the copied text; HL = past the name.
  Error: Z, A = 0 when the text is full.
  Changes: AF, B, DE, HL.
  Keeps: C, IX, IY.
  .165E The next text byte the ":" end mark?

@1662 COPY_NAME_SKIP
  Counts the name character; the end returns NZ (A is not 0 here).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Comparing a directory entry with a name

@1670 DIR_ENT_MATCH
  Compares the directory entry at DE with the 11-byte name at HL: "?" in the name matches any
  character, letters match in either case, and bit 7 is ignored. When IX+6 bit 3 asks for the
  volume label, any name matches. The entry's volume bit (attribute bit 3) must agree with IX+6
  bit 3.
  Entry: DE = the directory entry; HL = the name; IX = the request block.
  Exit: C set when it matches: A = the entry's attribute byte, HL = the entry + $0B.
  Error: NC: Z when the entry is free (A = 0, the end of the directory, or $E5, deleted), NZ
  when it does not match.
  Changes: AF, BC, HL.
  Keeps: DE, IX, IY.
  .1670 The end of the directory?
  .1673 A deleted entry?
  .1677 11 characters
  .1679 C = the request's flags (bit 3: volume label)
  .167C $05 in an entry stands for a first character $E5
  .1680 Compare $E5 then

@1682 DIR_MATCH_LP
  Compares one character.
  .1682 Volume label: any name
  .1686 The same, bit 7 aside?
  .168B Only the case differs?
  .168F And a letter?

@1693 DIR_MATCH_WILD
  "?" in the name matches anything.
  .1693 "?"?

@1698 DIR_MATCH_NEXT
  Steps to the next character; after 11 checks the volume bit.
  .169D HL = the entry's attribute byte
  .169F Both volume labels, or neither?
  .16A4 A = the attribute; C: a match
  .16A6 Skips OR A at $16A7, the no-match exit (NC)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The directory scan (state in IY+$32-$3B: $32 the cluster, $34 the next cluster, $36 the
# sector, $38 the sectors left, $39 the entries left in the sector, $3A the entries in it, $3B
# the entries of a last part-sector of the root)

@16AE DSCAN_ROOT_CHK
  The rest of the scan start at $16AA, with DE = the current directory's word (IY+$30): the
  root ($FFxx) takes its place from the drive parameter block, another directory is walked
  from its cluster.
  .16AE The root?
  .16B4 HL = the parameter block + $0F
  .16B8 C = the root's last part-sector entries (+$0F)
  .16BA A = the root's whole sectors (+$10)
  .16BD DE = the root's first sector (+$12)
  .16C1 $FF + C for both cluster words: no next cluster

@16D0 DIR_NEXT_ENTRY
  Steps the directory scan to the next 32-byte entry, reading the next sector, and the next
  cluster from the FAT, when needed.
  Entry: DE = the current entry; HL = the drive parameter block; IY+$32-$3B = the scan state.
  Exit: NC; DE = the next entry; A = its first byte, Z when 0 (the end mark).
  Error: C set (NZ) when the directory has no more entries (its sectors or chain ended).
  Changes: AF, DE, all alternates; the scan state.
  Keeps: BC, HL, IX, IY.
  .16D1 The next entry: 32 bytes on

@16D4 DIR_STEP_ENTRY
  Moves DE on by BC and loads the current sector number.
  .16D5 HL = the entry + BC
  .16D7 DE = the sector (IY+$36)

@16DB DIR_STEP_COUNT
  Counts the entry; the sector's last one moves on to the next sector.
  .16DB A = the entries left in the sector - 1
  .16DF The sector done?
  .16E1 The sector buffer still valid (IY+$63 bit 2 clear)?
  .16E8 No: read the sector again

@16EA DIR_NEXT_SECTOR
  Releases the sector buffer and moves to the next sector of the cluster, then of the chain.
  .16EA IX = the drive parameter block
  .16F4 HL = the drive parameter block
  .16F5 The next sector
  .16F6 Sectors left in the cluster?
  .16FC A last part-sector (the root)?
  .1701 DE = the next cluster (IY+$34)

@1705 DIR_END_CHK
  $FFxx as the next cluster ends the directory: C set.
  .1705 No next cluster?
  .1707 C: the end of the directory

@170A DIR_NEXT_CLUST
  Enters cluster DE: finds the one after it in the FAT and its first sector.
  .170C DE = the next cluster from the FAT
  .170F BC = the next cluster
  .1714 DE = the cluster's first sector
  .1719 HL = the parameter block + $0A
  .171D A = the sectors in a cluster (the byte + 1)
  .1722 No last part-sector

@1724 DSCAN_SET_RUN
  Stores the part-sector count, the next cluster and the cluster.
  .1724 The last part-sector's entries
  .1728 The next cluster
  .172F The cluster

@1735 DSCAN_FULL_SECT
  A whole sector: 16 entries.

@1737 DSCAN_SECT_CHK
  No whole sectors left: the last part-sector, counted once.
  .1737 Whole sectors left?
  .173A B = the part-sector's entries
  .173D Counted once
  .1740 One sector

@1741 DSCAN_STORE
  Stores the sector count, the sector and its entry count, then reads the sector.
  .1741 The sectors left
  .1744 The sector
  .174A The entries in the sector

@174F DSCAN_READ
  Reads the sector and points DE at the entry the count gives.
  .174F IX = the drive parameter block
  .1751 Keep the entries left
  .1752 One sector
  .1754 Read it: HL = the buffer, the data at HL + $0B
  .1757 B = the entries left
  .175A Back $15: the first entry is $20 on
  .175E A = the entry's place in the sector, from 1

@1766 DSCAN_INDEX_LP
  Steps HL on $20 bytes per entry.

@176F DSCAN_ENTRY
  Stores the entries left and returns the entry.
  .176F The entries left in the sector
  .1772 DE = the entry
  .1775 A = its first byte; Z when 0 (the end mark)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Function table entries ($0550)

@1778 FN_PARSE_SPEC
  Function 1 (table $0552): parses a file specification (drive, path and name) for the request
  block at DE without reading the disk (C bits 2 and 7 cleared), and unless C bit 0 is set
  copies the parsed 11-byte name into the block at +8.
  Entry: HL = the specification text (in the caller's segment); DE = the request block; C bit
  0 = do not copy the name.
  Exit: A = 0; IX = the block.
  Error: A = the parse's error code, NZ.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, IX, all alternates.
  Keeps: HL.
  .177A IX = the request block
  .177D No IY+$5E check, no disk
  .1781 HL = the block, DE = the text
  .1782 Parse the specification
  .1785 L = the caller's C
  .1788 Name wanted?
  .178D DE = the block's name (+8)
  .1795 HL = the parsed 11-byte name

@179E PARSE_SPEC_OK
  Returns A = 0.

@179F PARSE_SPEC_EXIT
  Restores HL and returns.

@17A1 CHANGE_DIR
  Function 2 (table $0554): resolves a path on the disk and makes it the drive's current
  directory: its cluster word goes to the drive block's +$1E and its path text to +$20.
  Entry: HL = the path text (in the caller's segment).
  Exit: A = 0; HL = the path text at IY+$06DE.
  Error: A = the parse's error code, or $A8 ("Invalid pathname string") when C is not 0 after
  the parse, NZ.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates.
  .17A1 DE = the path text
  .17A3 Search the disk (bit 7), flag bit 0
  .17A5 Parse and walk the path with the work block
  .17A9 Anything left in C?
  .17AD HL = the drive block + $1E
  .17B1 DE = the current directory's word (IY+$30)

@17B5 CHDIR_STORE
  Stores the directory word and copies the path text after it.
  .17B9 DE = the drive block's path text (+$20)
  .17BA HL = the path text just built

@17BD CHDIR_COPY_LP
  Copies the path text up to and with its 0.
  .17C0 The 0 copied?

@17C3 PATH_TEXT_ADDR
  Points HL at the path text buffer (IY+$06DE, 64 bytes, ended by 0).
  Entry: IY = the work area.
  Exit: HL = IY + $06DE.
  Changes: F, HL.
  Keeps: A, BC, DE, IX, IY.

@17CD GET_CUR_DIR
  Function 3 (table $0556): walks drive B's stored current directory again (an empty path) and
  returns its path text.
  Entry: B = the drive.
  Exit: A = 0; HL = the path text at IY+$06DE.
  Error: A = $AF ("Invalid drive") with Z; or the walk's error code, NZ.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates.
  .17CE A = the drive, 0 when there is none
  .17D3 No such drive?
  .17D7 Search the disk
  .17D9 An empty path text
  .17DC Walk the drive's stored path

@17E2 MAKE_DIR
  Function 7 (table $055E): creates a directory (attribute $10) through DIR_CREATE_ENT.
  Entry: DE = the request block (the name at +8); BC = the result buffer.
  Exit: as DIR_CREATE_ENT.
  Error: A = $A1 ("Invalid file attributes") for a volume label request (IX+6 bit 3), NZ; or
  DIR_CREATE_ENT's errors.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates.
  .17E5 A volume label?
  .17EC The directory attribute, Z

@17EE DIR_CREATE_ENT
  Function 6 (table $055C) and the body of 4, 5 and 7: finds the name of the request block in
  its directory and, when IY+$5D bit 7 is clear, creates an entry for it.
  1. Checks the name (CHECK_FILENAME) and stores the mode in IY+$5D: the attribute ($00 a
     file, $10 a directory), or $80 to find only (function 4 enters at $17F8, function 5 at
     DIR_SEARCH_ON).
  2. Resolves the path and starts the scan ($12CF), then searches (DIR_FIND_ENT).
  3. An existing entry is refused, or a file taken over (DELETE_ENTRY); a new one goes in the
     first free slot seen, or a new cluster of the directory; a directory gets a cleared
     cluster of its own.
  4. Writes $FE, the block's bytes 1-7 and the 32-byte entry to the result buffer (BC), saves
     the scan state at the block's +$17 and returns HL = the path text.
  Entry: A = the attribute and Z (the dispatcher passes A = 0); DE = the request block; BC = the
  result buffer (40 bytes).
  Exit: A = 0; DE = the block; HL = the path text at IY+$06DE.
  Error: A = $A6 ("Invalid file name"), $97 ("File exists"), $98 ("Directory exists"), $99
  ("System file exists"), $A7, $95, $AC ("Disk full"), $AD ("Root directory full"), $CF (not
  found, finding only), or the parse's error, NZ.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates.
  .17EE Keep the attribute and its Z in AF'
  .17F0 IX = the request block
  .17F2 A file name without wildcards?
  .17F6 A = the attribute, Z
  .17F7 Never jumps (Z here): hides LD A,$80 at $17F8, function 4's entry
  .17FA The mode: the attribute, or $80 to find only
  .17FD Resolve the path and start the scan
  .1801 NC: the scan goes on from the current entry
  .1802 Keep the result buffer's address

@1805 DIR_SEARCH_ON
  Function 5 (table $055A): goes on with the search that the request block's saved scan state
  (+$17) describes, from the entry after the last one found.
  Entry: DE = the request block; BC = the result buffer.
  Exit: A = 0; the next matching entry in the result buffer (as DIR_CREATE_ENT).
  Error: A = $CF when no more entries match, or the disk's error, NZ.
  Changes: AF, DE, HL, IX, all alternates.
  Keeps: BC.
  .1805 The mode: find only
  .1809 Restore the saved scan state
  .180D Keep the result buffer's address
  .180E The entry after the last one found

@1811 DIR_FIND_ENT
  Searches the directory; a match goes to FIND_FOUND, no match finds a free slot or fails.
  .1811 C: a match
  .1816 Finding only?
  .181A Not found when finding only
  .181C Creating: back to the free slot
  .181F Failed?

@1823 FIND_FOUND
  The name exists: when finding, return it; when creating, refuse it or take the file over.
  .1823 Finding only? Return the entry
  .1829 A system file (attribute bit 2)?
  .182D A directory?
  .182F "Directory exists"
  .1833 Creating a directory?
  .1837 "File exists"
  .1839 Never jumps (NC here): hides LD A,$99 at $183A, reached from $182B
  .183C A file: delete the old entry to reuse it
  .183F Refused?

@1841 CREATE_NEW
  Builds the attribute; a directory gets a new cleared cluster.
  .1841 A directory being made?
  .1846 BC = 0: no cluster for a file
  .1848 The archive bit
  .184C A cluster for the directory, cleared
  .1855 The directory attribute

@1853 CREATE_ALLOC_CHK
  Fails when the allocation (or the free slot) failed.

@1857 CREATE_WRITE
  Writes the new entry: the block's name, the attribute (the volume bit from IX+6), the
  cluster and the time.
  .1858 Read the current entry again: DE
  .185B HL = the request block
  .1861 The volume bit from the request
  .1867 HL = the block's name (+8)
  .186C Write the directory entry

@186F FIND_RET_ENTRY
  Success: A = 0.

@1870 FIND_COPY_OUT
  Fills the result buffer: $FE, the block's bytes 1-7, then the 32-byte entry.
  .1870 HL = the result buffer
  .1874 DE = the request block
  .1877 $FE first
  .187B The block's bytes 1-7
  .1882 The entry's first byte
  .1883 $05 stands for $E5

@1889 FIND_COPY_REST
  Copies the first byte and the other 31.
  .188C The other 31 bytes

@1891 FIND_SAVE_SCAN
  Saves the scan state at the block's +$17 and returns with the path text.
  .1891 HL = IY+$30, DE = IX+$17, BC = 12
  .1897 DE = the request block
  .189A Restore BC
  .189B HL = the path text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Checking and writing names

@189E CHECK_FILENAME
  Checks the request block's 8.3 name: the name may not start with a space, and no field may
  hold a separator, "?" or "*", or a character after a space. A volume label is not checked.
  Entry: IX = the request block (+6 bit 3 volume label, +8 the 11-byte name).
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: A = $A6 ("Invalid file name"), NZ.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .189E A volume label?
  .18A5 HL = the block's name (+8)
  .18AC 8 name characters; it may not start with a space
  .18B3 3 extension characters, which may all be spaces

@18BB CHECK_NAME_FLD
  Checks one field of B characters at HL.
  Entry: A = the character the field may not start with; B = the field length; HL = the field.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; HL = past the field.
  Error: A = $A6 ("Invalid file name"), NZ.
  Changes: AF, B, HL.
  Keeps: C, DE, IX, IY.
  .18BB The first character not allowed?

@18BE CHECK_NAME_LP
  Checks one character.
  .18C0 A space: only spaces may follow
  .18C4 A separator?
  .18C9 "?"?
  .18CD "*"?

@18D3 CHECK_NAME_OK
  Returns A = 0, Z.

@18D5 CHECK_NAME_PADLP
  Takes the next character after a space.

@18D7 CHECK_NAME_PAD
  The rest of the field must be spaces.
  .18D7 Not a space?

@18DE CHECK_NAME_BAD
  Returns error $A6 ("Invalid file name").

@18E2 WRITE_DIR_ENTRY
  Writes a new directory entry: the 11-byte name (a first byte $E5 stored as $05), the
  attribute, zeros, the start cluster, then the time and date; marks the sector buffer
  changed.
  Entry: DE = the entry; HL = the name; A = the attribute; BC = the start cluster (bit 7 of B
  set: none, 0 is written).
  Exit: AF and AF' swapped ($190C).
  Changes: AF, BC, HL, AF'.
  Keeps: DE, IX, IY.
  .18E4 B = the attribute
  .18E6 $E5 would read as deleted?
  .18EA It is stored as $05

@18EC WRITE_ENT_NAME
  Stores the name and the attribute.
  .18F0 The other 10 name bytes
  .18F5 The attribute (+$0B)
  .18F7 20 bytes, +$0C to +$1F

@18F9 WRITE_ENT_ZERO
  Clears the rest of the entry (C = 0 after the copy).

@1904 WRITE_ENT_CLUST
  Stores the start cluster at +$1A and stamps the time.
  .1904 HL = the entry + $1B
  .1908 The start cluster
  .190B Restore DE
  .190C Swap in AF'

@190D STAMP_ENTRY
  Writes the current time (+$16) and date (+$18) into the directory entry at DE and marks the
  sector buffer changed.
  Entry: DE = the directory entry.
  Exit: none.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .1910 HL = the entry's time field
  .1917 The sector buffer changed

@191E PACK_TIME_DATE
  Reads the clock (EXOS 32 and 34) and writes the time and the date in the disk's packed
  form: hours x 2048 + minutes x 32 + seconds / 2, then (year - 1980) x 512 + month x 32 +
  day, each least significant byte first.
  Entry: HL = 4 bytes.
  Exit: the 4 bytes written; HL = the last.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .1920 C = the hours, binary
  .1925 A = the minutes, binary
  .1929 Minutes x 4
  .192B Three more shifts into C

@192D PACK_HOURS_LP
  Shifts the minutes' top bits into C: C:A = hours x 2048 + minutes x 32; then the seconds
  and the date.
  .1932 D = the low byte so far
  .1933 A = the seconds, binary
  .1937 Seconds / 2
  .193A The time, low byte first
  .193D HL = the date's place
  .1942 C = the years, binary
  .1947 A = the month, binary
  .194B Month bits 0-2 to bits 5-7, bit 3 to carry
  .194F C = years x 2 + month bit 3
  .1952 A = the day, binary
  .1957 The date, low byte first

@195B BCD_TO_BIN
  Turns a BCD byte into binary: tens x 10 + units.
  Entry: A = the BCD byte.
  Exit: A = its value.
  Changes: AF, B.
  Keeps: C, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .195C B = tens x 8
  .1960 Tens x 2
  .1962 + tens x 8
  .1965 + the units

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Searching a directory for the request block's name

@1969 SEARCH_NEXT
  Steps to the next entry first.

@196C SEARCH_DIR
  Searches the rest of the directory for the request block's name, remembering the first free
  slot (a deleted entry, or the end mark) when none is saved yet.
  Entry: C set when the directory has already ended; DE = the current entry; HL = the drive
  parameter block; IX = the request block.
  Exit: C set: DE = the matching entry, A = its attribute.
  Error: NC, A = 0, Z: no match before the end.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, all alternates; the scan state; IY+$3C-$47 and IY+$5D bit 0.
  Keeps: HL, IX, IY.
  .196C The end of the directory?
  .1970 HL = the block's name (+8)
  .1975 Does the entry match?
  .1979 Yes: C
  .197A An entry in use: the next
  .197C A free slot saved already?
  .1980 No: save this one
  .1983 A deleted entry: go on; 0 is the end

@1986 SEARCH_NONE
  Returns A = 0, NC: not found.

@1988 SAVE_FREE_SLOT
  Saves the scan state (IY+$30-$3B) at IY+$3C as the free slot and sets IY+$5D bit 0.
  Entry: IY = the work area.
  Exit: none.
  Changes: F, BC.
  Keeps: A, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .198D HL = IY+$30, DE = IY+$3C

@199A USE_FREE_SLOT
  Moves the scan back to the saved free slot; without one, adds a cluster to the directory
  and moves into it. The sector buffer must be read again (IY+$63 bit 2).
  Entry: HL = the drive parameter block; IX = the request block.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; the scan state at the free slot (or at the new cluster's first entry).
  Error: A = $AD ("Root directory full") or $AC ("Disk full"), NZ.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, all alternates.
  Keeps: HL, IX, IY.
  .199E A free slot saved?
  .19A8 HL = IY+$3C, DE = IY+$30

@19B0 GROW_DIR
  No free slot: the directory needs a new cluster.
  .19B0 DE = the directory's cluster (IY+$32)

@19B4 GROW_DIR_SUB
  The root cannot grow; another directory gets a cleared cluster linked after this one.
  .19B4 The root?
  .19B9 A cleared cluster: BC
  .19BE Link it after cluster DE in the FAT
  .19C2 The next cluster
  .19C8 Step into it

@19CB ALLOC_CLEAR_1
  Allocates one cluster and clears it (IY+$48 bit 1), through ALLOC_CLUSTERS.
  Entry: HL = the drive parameter block.
  Exit: A = 0; BC = the new cluster.
  Error: A = $AC ("Disk full"), NZ.
  Changes: AF, BC, HL, all alternates.
  Keeps: DE, IX.
  .19CB One cluster
  .19CE Clear it

@19D2 ALLOC_CLUSTERS
  Allocates BC free clusters as a chain, the search starting from the cluster that FAT entry 1
  holds (the search goes on at $19DD); IY+$48 bit 1 asks for each to be cleared.
  Entry: BC = the clusters wanted; HL = the drive parameter block.
  Exit: A = 0; BC = the new chain's first cluster.
  Error: A = $AC ("Disk full"), NZ.
  Changes: AF, BC, HL, all alternates.
  Keeps: DE, IX.
  .19D3 FAT entry 1
  .19D6 DE = its value

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

@1AE9 DELETE_ENTRY
  Deletes the current directory entry: frees its cluster chain and marks it deleted ($E5,
  its first byte kept at +$0C).
  Entry: DE = the current entry; HL = the drive parameter block.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: A = $A7 ("Read only file") for attribute bit 0; A = $95 ("File cannot be copied onto
  itself") when IY-$37 bit 1 is set and its drive (IY+$2B) and start cluster are those at
  IY+$60-$62; NZ.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, all alternates.
  Keeps: HL, IX, IY.
  .1AE9 Read the entry again: DE
  .1AED HL = the attribute byte
  .1AF2 Read only?
  .1AFA HL = the start cluster's place (+$1A)
  .1AFE DE = the start cluster
  .1B01 The file open for a copy?
  .1B07 The same drive?
  .1B0F The same start cluster?

question 17A9 What does C hold after the parse $1356 returns, which CHANGE_DIR requires to be 0?
question 19D2 Which cluster does ALLOC_CLUSTERS return in BC: the chain's first, as GROW_DIR uses it?
question 1778 Does function 1 really parse without reading the disk (C bit 7 cleared)?

